AHS-015 — Interface Architecture

Document ID: AHS-015
Title: Interface Architecture
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Logical, Implementation

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical Interface Architecture of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

It establishes standardized contracts for interaction between Atlas components, services, external systems, and human interfaces while ensuring modularity, interoperability, extensibility, and technology independence.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Interface principles
Interface contracts
Interface categories
Interface lifecycle
Compatibility requirements
Interface governance

This specification does not define transport protocols, serialization formats, or programming language bindings.

3. Canonical Definition

An Interface is a formal contract that defines how Atlas components exchange information, invoke capabilities, and coordinate behavior without exposing internal implementation details.

4. Interface Objectives

Atlas interfaces SHALL:

Hide implementation details.
Expose only required capabilities.
Preserve canonical engineering artifacts.
Support loose coupling.
Enable interoperability.
Maintain backward compatibility where practical.
Support version evolution.
5. Interface Principles

Every interface SHALL follow these principles:

Contract First

Interfaces SHALL be defined before implementation.

Technology Independence

Interfaces SHALL not depend on specific programming languages or frameworks.

Canonical Artifacts

Interfaces SHALL exchange Canonical Engineering Artifacts (CEA) wherever applicable.

Explicit Contracts

Inputs, outputs, errors, and constraints SHALL be explicitly defined.

Stable Semantics

Behavior SHALL remain semantically consistent across compatible versions.

Minimal Exposure

Interfaces SHALL expose only the capabilities necessary for their intended purpose.

6. Interface Categories

Atlas defines six canonical interface categories.

6.1 Cognitive Interfaces

Expose cognitive capabilities.

Examples:

Understanding Service
Knowledge Service
Reasoning Service
Capability Service
6.2 Governance Interfaces

Provide governance functions.

Examples:

Policy evaluation
Context management
Constraint validation
Confidence assessment
Explainability services
6.3 Platform Interfaces

Support runtime operations.

Examples:

Workflow management
Audit logging
Identity services
Configuration services
Monitoring services
6.4 Integration Interfaces

Connect Atlas with external systems.

Examples:

CAD
BIM
GIS
ERP
PLM
IoT platforms
Digital Twin platforms
6.5 Human Interfaces

Enable interaction with users.

Examples:

Engineering dashboards
Review workbenches
Decision support portals
Administrative consoles
6.6 Administration Interfaces

Support operational management.

Examples:

Health monitoring
Diagnostics
Configuration
Maintenance
Deployment management
7. Canonical Interface Contract

Every interface SHALL define:

Contract Element	Description
Interface ID	Globally unique identifier
Name	Human-readable identifier
Purpose	Functional responsibility
Owner	Responsible component/service
Inputs	Accepted artifacts
Outputs	Produced artifacts
Preconditions	Required conditions
Postconditions	Expected outcomes
Constraints	Applicable limitations
Errors	Defined failure conditions
Version	Interface version
Security Requirements	Authentication and authorization expectations
8. Interface Interaction Model
Consumer
    │
    ▼
Interface Contract
    │
    ▼
Provider

Consumers SHALL interact only through the interface contract.

Direct access to internal implementations SHALL NOT occur.

9. Interface Lifecycle

Every interface SHALL follow the lifecycle:

Proposed
    │
Reviewed
    │
Approved
    │
Published
    │
Implemented
    │
Operational
    │
Deprecated
    │
Retired

Lifecycle transitions SHALL be governed through version control.

10. Versioning

Every interface SHALL include:

Major version
Minor version
Patch version

Rules:

Major versions MAY introduce incompatible changes.
Minor versions SHALL remain backward compatible.
Patch versions SHALL correct defects without changing behavior.
11. Compatibility

Atlas interfaces SHALL define compatibility guarantees.

Supported compatibility categories:

Backward compatible
Forward compatible
Compatible with adapters
Incompatible

Compatibility requirements SHALL be documented.

12. Error Model

Every interface SHALL define standardized error categories.

Examples:

Invalid input
Missing artifacts
Constraint violation
Policy violation
Authorization failure
Processing failure
Timeout
Internal error

Errors SHALL include sufficient diagnostic information for recovery and audit.

13. Security Requirements

Interfaces SHALL specify:

Authentication requirements
Authorization requirements
Data confidentiality expectations
Integrity requirements
Audit obligations

Security SHALL be implementation-independent while remaining enforceable.

14. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Implements canonical interface contracts.
Preserves contract semantics.
Supports interface versioning.
Documents compatibility.
Uses Canonical Engineering Artifacts where applicable.
15. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Domain-specific interface profiles.
Machine-readable interface specifications.
Automated contract validation.
Dynamic service discovery.
Semantic interface negotiation.

Such extensions SHALL preserve the canonical interface model.